    "description": "Hansard of July 25, 2024",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1722840995013814.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-07-25-2024-07-25-0100",
    "chunk_index": 100,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "493c94c5e9f7669782923b8855fe28f4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1811,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Deputy Chairman of Committees, secondly, this Economic Transformation Bill should have had a much wider stakeholder discussion, a wider public dialogue. The Hon. President believed in the Harvard Kennedy School\u2019s Growth Lab approach. The Growth Lab approach speaks about public participation. He spoke on behalf of public participation in 2023. But, how much opportunity was given to exporters, local entrepreneurs and investors to express their views about this Economic Transformation Bill? There was not sufficient public discussion. Therefore, there should be more public consultation before a Bill like this is brought into force.\n\nAnother thing which the Opposition opposes in this Bill is, the process of appointing directors or members to the five agencies in the Economic Transformation Bill. These appointments are made by the Minister at his discretion. This is just like the National Electricity Advisory Council - we opposed it - to which the Minister could appoint members without any checks and balances. These key appointments must go through the Constitutional Council, if there is to be any transparency and accountability. Therefore, there would be a lack of confidence among investors, if these appointments to the five key institutions proposed in this Bill are made by politicians. Take, for example, \u201cInvest Sri Lanka\u201d, which will be a public company. There are no key performance indicators for them at all. Without KPIs, these directors could be just lackeys of the Minister, who could do globetrotting without trying to achieve any targets at all. Therefore, we believe that the appointment of these key members, the directors, should go through the Constitutional Council and that there should be transparency and accountability in appointing those holding these positions."
  },
