    "chunk_size_bytes": 1670,
    "chunk_text": "Another thing which the Opposition opposes in this Bill is, the process of appointing directors or members to the five agencies in the Economic Transformation Bill. These appointments are made by the Minister at his discretion. This is just like the National Electricity Advisory Council - we opposed it - to which the Minister could appoint members without any checks and balances. These key appointments must go through the Constitutional Council, if there is to be any transparency and accountability. Therefore, there would be a lack of confidence among investors, if these appointments to the five key institutions proposed in this Bill are made by politicians. Take, for example, \u201cInvest Sri Lanka\u201d, which will be a public company. There are no key performance indicators for them at all. Without KPIs, these directors could be just lackeys of the Minister, who could do globetrotting without trying to achieve any targets at all. Therefore, we believe that the appointment of these key members, the directors, should go through the Constitutional Council and that there should be transparency and accountability in appointing those holding these positions.\n\nof the other companies which have BoI approval and which do not work within those Zones be addressed? Is it the Economic Commission or the \"Zones SL\"? I think there is an Amendment proposed, which states that they will give a transitional provision of three months, but that is not cited in the Bill very clearly. Right now, there are already 133 projects pending BOI approval. How would you manage them? What is the transitional provision the Government has provided in this Bill to accommodate them."
