    "md5": "e7e642f752c0fe2ff3f45739284f48ea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1661,
    "chunk_text": "You have to register that and get it enforced. Enforcement of the judgment means, if you want to go and seize a property or a bank account or even give a minor child\u2019s custody to the other party, a court order is required. So, that is how this is going to help those people. This is a long-felt need. I identified this in the year 2000 and immediately appointed two Subcommittees to go into those two areas.\n\nSo, we will continue to introduce such laws. I think there are a number of laws in the pipeline, which are a culmination of the work we started in the latter part of 2020, as I took over as the Minister of Justice. We managed to revisit about 88 laws. There were about 29 Subcommittees that we had appointed. All that was possible because the members of the legal profession volunteered to take ownership of that work because they thought, \"All this is part of our own law, it is for our own country and we must contribute to that\". So, I am very thankful to all those who helped us in drafting these laws, particularly the Attorney-General\u2019s Department for\n\n675 676\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nincorporating those amendments and looking into the Bills at their different stages and also the Legal Draftsman\u2019s Department for cooperating with us in incorporate those amendments. We know that when you draft a law, it goes up and down and that it is not an easy task. Therefore, I thought it fit to mention them here and thank them: the Subcommittee members, the Ministry Officials, the Legal Draftsman, the Attorney-General, and all those who got involved in drafting these laws and submitting them for the betterment of our country."
  },
