    "md5": "9964fd6709132dc3f085306758a60a04",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1220,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, this Bill, the Second Reading of which is scheduled to be taken up, proposes Amendments to the National Minimum Wage of Workers Act, No. 3 of 2016. The primary goal of this Amendment is to raise the current national minimum wage for private sector employees from Rs. 12,500 to Rs. 17,500. More than three million of the Sri Lankan workforce is currently employed in the private sector.\n\nPrior to the National Minimum Wage of Workers Act, No. 03 of 2016, wages for employees, particularly of those who were covered under the Shop and Office Employees (Regulation of Employment and Remuneration) Act, were primarily determined through agreements made at the time of recruitment of employees. Although unionized workers have been able to get their wages raised, the majority of the private sector workers are unable to negotiate with their employers to get their wages increased. In addition to that, the high rate of unemployment and prevalence of unskilled workers further hinder their ability to negotiate for better salaries upon hiring. That is why the Government intervened through legal provisions to protect these vulnerable workers by mandating wage increases.\n\n689 690\n\n2024 \u0dc3\u0d9a\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dad\u0d9a\u0dda\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 03"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2024-09-03-2024-09-03",
    "num": "2024-09-03",
    "date_str": "2024-09-03",
    "description": "Hansard of September 03, 2024",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
