    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1725872549048050.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-09-03-2024-09-03-0137",
    "chunk_index": 137,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "db27fb784245f23ef77253b13c9ae609",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1485,
    "chunk_text": "Prior to the National Minimum Wage of Workers Act, No. 03 of 2016, wages for employees, particularly of those who were covered under the Shop and Office Employees (Regulation of Employment and Remuneration) Act, were primarily determined through agreements made at the time of recruitment of employees. Although unionized workers have been able to get their wages raised, the majority of the private sector workers are unable to negotiate with their employers to get their wages increased. In addition to that, the high rate of unemployment and prevalence of unskilled workers further hinder their ability to negotiate for better salaries upon hiring. That is why the Government intervened through legal provisions to protect these vulnerable workers by mandating wage increases.\n\n689 690\n\n2024 \u0dc3\u0d9a\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dad\u0d9a\u0dda\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 03\n\nSir, due to the recent economic crisis, the cost of living has increased significantly. Yet, there has been no corresponding raise in the minimum wage of the private sector employees since 2021. It has been observed that many employers currently pay a minimum wage of Rs. 16,000 to employees in the security service, cleaning service and small shops and offices. This amount is insufficient, given the current cost of living. According to the Department of Census and Statistics, the Official Poverty Line has risen to Rs. 16, 326 by May, 2024. This indicates that the national minimum wage for private sector employees is below the Official Poverty Line."
  },
