    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "126d8389e605f9ec278e3826e97e214a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1497,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, first of all, I would like to say something about the Provincial Councils Elections (Amendment) Bill, which had gone through its First Reading, the Second Reading and was scheduled to be taken up in Parliament today for its Third Reading. Unfortunately, it has not been permitted to be taken up in Parliament today. It is very unfortunate because, as we know, the provincial councils have not been in operation for quite some time now and that is really an issue about democracy. I do not agree with the Hon. M.A. Sumanthiran when he says that the Provincial Councils Elections (Amendment) Bill not\n\nbeing taken up today is an injustice perpetrated on the Tamil-speaking people because that is actually an injustice perpetrated on all Sri Lankans.\n\nI heard some Hon. Members say that if the Provincial Councils Elections (Amendment) Bill was taken up today, it would have created some problems because we are on the brink of an election. I do not agree with that at all because these Bills go through a certain, long process: the White Paper goes to the Attorney-General's Department, then, to the Legal Draftsman's Department and after the First and the Second Readings, the Bill goes to the Legislative Standing Committee and then only it comes up for the Third Reading. That is how it is passed. So, this particular Bill has also gone through that whole process and that is why we completely object to what the Government did today by not putting it before the House for its Third Reading."
  },
